1 NOTE GENERATION 1 FAMILY (1821-1882). Petrus van Duijnhoven (1799-1882) x Johanna van der Heijden (1798-1875). Married 24 Feb 1821 Uden. Had 12 children over ~20 years, but only 6 survived to adulthood (50% childhood mortality). Surviving children: Johanna (1822-1896, @I049@), Anna Maria (1823-1901, @I050@), Petronella (1825-1902, @I051@), Martinus (1829-1903, @I003@, our direct ancestor), Henrica (1833-1910, @I052@), Hendricus (1837-1910, @I053@), and Johannes (~1832, @I054@, married Ardina Verwegen). This family lived through the Napoleonic aftermath, the Belgian Revolution (1830), the devastating Potato Famine (1845-1847), and the joyous restoration of the Catholic hierarchy (1853). They established the Van Duijnhoven presence in Uden that continues to this day.

0 @I003@ INDI
1 NAME Martinus /van Duijnhoven/
2 GIVN Martinus
2 SURN van Duijnhoven
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 18 DEC 1829
2 PLAC Uden, Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 JUL 1903
2 PLAC Uden, Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
1 OCCU Akkerbouwer (arable farmer)
2 PLAC Uden, Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE GENERATION 2 HEAD (18 Dec 1829 - 18 Jul 1903). Born during the reign of King Willem I, just one year before the Belgian Revolution that saw much of Catholic Brabant's southern neighbors secede. Martinus remained on the Dutch side of the border, in Uden.
2 CONT 
2 CONT OCCUPATION: Almost certainly an "akkerbouwer" (arable farmer) in Uden, following his father Petrus's occupation as a bouwman. The distinction is subtle: bouwman suggests a general yeoman farmer, while akkerbouwer specifically emphasizes field crop cultivation. Both terms indicate an independent small farmer rather than a landless laborer. Martinus would have cultivated the traditional Brabant crops on the sandy essen: rye, buckwheat, potatoes, and by mid-century increasingly sugar beets as the sugar industry developed.
2 CONT 
2 CONT HISTORICAL CONTEXT OF HIS LIFE:
2 CONT - Born during the Batavian Republic's aftermath; raised in the newly independent Kingdom of the Netherlands
2 CONT - Age 1 when Belgian Revolution erupted (1830) — Brabant remained Dutch
2 CONT - Age 15-17 during the catastrophic POTATO FAMINE (1845-1847). As a teenager, he witnessed his parents struggle to feed the family when the potato harvest failed by 71% and rye by 47%
2 CONT - Age 18 during CONSTITUTIONAL REFORM (1848) — brought religious freedom to Catholics
2 CONT - Age 23 when CATHOLIC HIERARCHY RESTORED (4 March 1853). This was the defining religious event of his generation. Pope Pius IX's bull "Ex qua die arcano" established the Diocese of 's-Hertogenbosch. For Brabant Catholics, 262 years of clandestine worship ended. Bishop Joannes Zwijsen became the first Archbishop of Utrecht since the Reformation.
2 CONT - Age 28 when he married Geertruda Verwegen (17 Feb 1858 Uden)
2 CONT - Age 35-40 during the RAILROAD ERA (1860s) — rail lines transformed Brabant's isolation
2 CONT - Age 48-65 during the AGRICULTURAL CRISIS (1878-1895). Cheap American grain flooded European markets after prairie cultivation expanded and steamship transport costs plummeted. Dutch grain prices collapsed. Many small farmers in Brabant diversified into dairy, pigs, or market gardening.
2 CONT 
2 CONT MARRIAGE: Married Geertruda Verwegen on 17 Feb 1858 in Uden. Geertruda was daughter of Johannes Verwegen and Mechelina van den Berk. Notably, Martinus's brother Johannes had married Ardina Verwegen (likely Geertruda's sister) two years earlier on 26 Jan 1856 — the two Verwegen sisters married two Van Duijnhoven brothers, a common pattern of family alliance in tight-knit Catholic Brabant villages.
2 CONT 
2 CONT CHILDREN: He and Geertruda had at least 9 children: Lambertus (1861), Maria (1864), Antonius (1865), Petrus Cornelus (1867), Petronella (1869), Johanna (died young ~1870), and MARIANUS (24 Apr 1872) — our direct ancestor who continued the main line. High child mortality struck this family too: several children died young. Of his male children, only Marianus is confirmed to have survived to adulthood and continued the line — making him the sole surviving son.
2 CONT 
2 CONT WIDOWERHOOD: Geertruda died circa 1900, leaving Martinus a widower for his final 3 years.
2 CONT 
2 CONT DEATH: Martinus died 18 Jul 1903 in Uden at age 73 years, 7 months. He had seen Brabant transform from a marginal heathland region to productive farmland, witnessed his faith emerge from hiding, survived agricultural crises, and raised his son Marianus who would carry the family into the 20th century.
