1 MILI US Military Veteran
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE Generation 4 emigrant who established the American branch. Eighth child of Marianus and Anna Maria. Born in Uden 12 Nov 1915. US Military veteran. Married Anna Maria Cornelissen in Bakel en Milheeze 5 Oct 1950 — just weeks before emigrating. Departed Southampton 16 Nov 1950 aboard RMS Queen Elizabeth (Cunard Line), at 83,673 gross tons the LARGEST OCEAN LINER IN THE WORLD at that time, arriving New York via Cherbourg. The Queen Elizabeth carried thousands of European emigrants to America in the post-war years; Johan and Anna Maria were among the final wave before jet travel made ocean crossings obsolete. Johan was part of a massive Dutch post-war emigration: between 1946-1972, the Netherlands government actively encouraged over 500,000 citizens to emigrate due to fears of overpopulation and post-WWII economic hardship. The US Displaced Persons Act of 1948 and Refugee Relief Act of 1953 specifically facilitated Dutch Catholic emigration to rural Catholic communities in America. Johan settled in Green Isle, Sibley County, Minnesota — a small farming community named after Green Isle in County Galway, Ireland. The town was founded by Irish Catholic settlers brought to Minnesota by Archbishop John Ireland's Catholic Colonization Bureau in the 1870s-1880s; Dutch Catholic farmers like Johan found a welcoming home in this tight-knit faith community. St. Brendan's Catholic Church (built 1863), with its tall steeple visible across the prairie, became the spiritual center of Johan's new life. He farmed corn and soybeans in Sibley County through the 1950s-1980s, the era when these crops transformed the upper Midwest into America's breadbasket. Naturalized US citizen 5 Jun 1956 in Mankato, Blue Earth County MN (Certificate #7468861) — the same day as his wife Anna Maria. Anglicized name to "John van Duynhoven". Buried at St. Brendan's Cemetery, Green Isle (Plot 258A-c). Key figure linking Dutch and American branches.
