1 NOTE Generation 3. Daughter of Martinus van Duijnhoven (Gen 2) and Geertruda Verwegen, born circa 1870 in Uden. Sister of Marianus (1872, direct ancestor). Died young around age 10 (circa 1880). Childhood mortality was tragically common in 19th-century Brabant, where infectious diseases like diphtheria, typhoid, scarlet fever, and tuberculosis claimed many children before modern medicine and sanitation. An earlier daughter named Johanna (sometimes recorded as Lambertina) also died young, reflecting the era's practice of reusing names of deceased children - a poignant reminder of how often families lost children in infancy or childhood. The Catholic faith provided comfort through beliefs in infant baptism ensuring salvation and reunion in heaven. Johanna's short life was part of the broader story of her generation: of 9 known children of Martinus and Geertruda, most survived to adulthood, but losses like Johanna were the painful reality of the era. Confirmed via FamilySearch (GWW8-NNX) and BHIC civil records.
