1 DEAT
2 DATE 20 AUG 1990
2 PLAC Red Wing, Goodhue County, Minnesota, USA
1 BURI
2 PLAC Calvary Cemetery, Red Wing, Minnesota, USA
2 NOTE FindAGrave Memorial 94460049
1 MILI US Military Veteran
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE Mary Cecilia Campbell's father. US military veteran. Father of 16 children with Mary Ellen Ortscheid (15 reached adulthood — one infant Jerome died). Fairman raised his large family in Red Wing, Minnesota — a historic Mississippi River city incorporated in 1857, named after a Dakota Sioux chief (Hupahuduta, meaning "red wing"), and famous for two industries: Red Wing Pottery (founded 1861 when German immigrant John Paul discovered rich clay deposits) and Red Wing Shoes (founded 1905 by Charles Beckman). Red Wing sits at the foot of Barn Bluff where the Mississippi widens into Lake Pepin, making it one of Minnesota's most scenic river towns. The city thrived as a prosperous factory town through the 1950s, with workers in clay industries, shoe factories, tanneries, malting operations, and flour mills. Fairman and Mary Ellen's 15 surviving children — Marion, Joyce, Theresa, Patricia, Mary Margaret, Mary Cecilia, Marie, Barbara, James, Wayne, John, Joseph, William (Bill), Michael, Thomas, and Steven — grew up in this tight-knit Catholic community.
1 FAMS @F024@

0 @I118@ INDI
1 NAME Mary Ellen /Ortscheid/
2 GIVN Mary Ellen
2 SURN Ortscheid
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 4 JUL 1919
2 PLAC Cuba City, Grant County, Wisconsin, USA
1 DEAT
2 DATE 8 NOV 2012
2 PLAC The Lighthouse at Waconia, Minnesota, USA
2 NOTE Age 93
1 BURI
2 PLAC Calvary Cemetery, Red Wing, Minnesota, USA
1 EDUC Northern Illinois State Teachers College, DeKalb
2 DATE 1940
2 NOTE Teaching degree
1 OCCU Teacher
1 OCCU Reading tutor and librarian
2 PLAC Minnesota Correctional Facility - Red Wing
2 NOTE Retired 1974
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE Mary Cecilia Campbell's mother. Born 4 July 1919 in Cuba City, Wisconsin — a small town in Grant County at the heart of the Upper Mississippi Valley Mining District, where lead and zinc mining had dominated the local economy since the 1820s. The mining district was the most important lead-producing area in the United States from 1830-1871, and Cuba City remained surrounded by active zinc-lead mines through the early 1900s. Mary Ellen left this mining town background to attend Northern Illinois State Teachers College in DeKalb (now Northern Illinois University) — established in 1895 as a teacher-training institution that was empowered to award four-year Bachelor of Education degrees in 1921. She earned her teaching degree around 1940. After marrying Fairman Campbell, she moved to Red Wing, Minnesota where she worked as a teacher, reading tutor, and librarian at the Minnesota Correctional Facility - Red Wing — the state's second oldest correctional institution, established in 1866 as the House of Refuge and relocated to Red Wing in 1891. The facility provided education and rehabilitation for juvenile offenders, and Mary Ellen served there until her retirement in 1974. Mother of 16 children (15 reached adulthood). Daughter of Joseph Ortscheid and Teresa McDonald. Lived to age 93.
1 FAMC @F025@
1 FAMS @F024@

0 @I119@ INDI
1 NAME Joseph /Ortscheid/
2 GIVN Joseph
2 SURN Ortscheid
1 SEX M
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE Mary Ellen Ortscheid's father, Mary Cecilia Campbell's maternal grandfather. Lived in Cuba City, Grant County, Wisconsin — a small town at the heart of the Upper Mississippi Valley Mining District. Lead mining in this region of the Wisconsin Driftless Area began in the 1820s, and from 1830-1871 the district was the most important lead-producing area in the United States. By the early 1900s, the area had shifted to zinc mining, with numerous mines operating around Cuba City (Raisbeck, Pittsburg, Dall, Gritty Six, and others). The Ortscheid family was part of the German-Catholic community that settled in southwestern Wisconsin alongside the mining industry. Joseph and his wife Teresa McDonald raised their daughter Mary Ellen in this mining town background before she left to attend teachers college in DeKalb, Illinois. Spouse family research — connects to Gen 6.
1 FAMS @F025@

0 @I120@ INDI
1 NAME Teresa /McDonald/
2 GIVN Teresa
2 SURN McDonald
1 SEX F
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE Mary Ellen Ortscheid's mother, Mary Cecilia Campbell's maternal grandmother. The McDonald surname suggests Irish-Catholic ancestry — many Irish families settled in the lead-mining regions of southwestern Wisconsin alongside German Catholics like the Ortscheids. The Irish had been drawn to lead mining since the 1820s, and by the late 19th century the region had substantial Irish-Catholic and German-Catholic communities who often intermarried. Teresa and Joseph Ortscheid raised their daughter Mary Ellen in Cuba City, Grant County, Wisconsin. Spouse family research — connects to Gen 6.
1 FAMS @F025@

