1 NAME Geurt Peter /Verwegen/
2 GIVN Geurt Peter
2 SURN Verwegen
1 SEX M
1 OCCU Farmer/Agricultural worker
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE Johannes Verwegen's father, Geertruda Verwegen's paternal grandfather. Married Helena (Leen) Jan Gielens on 31 January 1773 in Boekel — a small village in the eastern part of Noord-Brabant that became an independent parish in 1677. Boekel was part of the Catholic heerlijkheid (lordship) of Ravenstein until 1794, which meant it enjoyed religious freedom while the rest of the Dutch Republic was officially Protestant. The Franciscan monastery Huize Padua was established here in 1742, benefiting from this religious tolerance. In 1773, when Geurt Peter and Leen married, Boekel was a tight-knit farming community where Catholic families like the Verwegens intermarried frequently with other local Catholic families. This marriage is particularly notable because the Verwegen family became deeply intertwined with the Van Duijnhoven family through multiple cross-marriages: Geertruda Verwegen married Martinus van Duijnhoven (Gen 2), and Ardina Verwegen (likely Geertruda's sister) married Johannes van Duijnhoven (Martinus's brother) — two Verwegen sisters married two Van Duijnhoven brothers, a remarkable family arrangement common in these close-knit Catholic Brabant villages. Gen -1 on Verwegen line.
1 FAMS @F027@

0 @I124@ INDI
