1 NOTE Generation -7. Son of Johannes "Jan Dirkse" van Duynhoven (1539, Gen -8). Father of Jan Dircx van Duynhoven (Gen -6). The patronym "Janssen" confirms his father was Johannes/Jan. HISTORICAL CONTEXT: Born around 1569 - the year after the Eighty Years War erupted - Dirck grew up during the most violent phase of the Dutch Revolt. His childhood saw Spanish troops under the Duke of Alba terrorize Brabant with the Council of Blood (1567-1573). He lived through the Union of Utrecht (1579) that created the Dutch Republic, which left Catholic Brabant in limbo between Spanish and Dutch control. The BHIC burial record (28 Feb 1692 in Erp) suggests remarkable longevity if accurate - possibly 123 years - but more likely indicates a birth date around 1620s rather than 1569, or this may be a different Dirck. Erp, like Aarle-Rixtel, was a village in the Meierij van 's-Hertogenbosch, which after 1648 became part of the Generality Lands - Catholic territory ruled as a colonial possession by the Protestant Dutch Republic. The Van Duijnhoven family weathered this transition, maintaining their Catholic faith through generations of religious restrictions. Source: Van Elk genealogy tree I6920.php.
1 FAMC @F040@
1 FAMS @F041@

